          </div>
          </ParamsDrawer>

          <div class="widget-column widget-column--results">
          <div class="results card">
            <h3>Results</h3>
            <div class="result-grid" id="nlc-results">
              <div class="result-item">
                <span class="result-label">Output wavelength</span>
                <span class="result-value" id="nlc-out-lam">&mdash;</span>
              </div>
              <div class="result-item">
                <span class="result-label">Output energy</span>
                <span class="result-value" id="nlc-out-ev">&mdash;</span>
              </div>
              <div class="result-item" id="nlc-sig-item" style="display:none">
                <span class="result-label">Signal &lambda;<sub>s</sub></span>
                <span class="result-value" id="nlc-out-sig">&mdash;</span>
              </div>
              <div class="result-item" id="nlc-idl-item" style="display:none">
                <span class="result-label">Idler &lambda;<sub>i</sub></span>
                <span class="result-value" id="nlc-out-idl">&mdash;</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="chart-wrap">
              <canvas id="nlc-chart" aria-label="Nonlinear conversion energy diagram"></canvas>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="notes-card card">
            <h4>Notes</h4>
            <p>
              <strong>Nonlinear conversion.</strong> Photon-energy bookkeeping for the
              harmonic and three-wave mixing processes: energy conservation fixes the
              output wavelength, and the photon energy follows from it&nbsp;[5].
            </p>
            <div id="nlc-note-variants">
              <div data-process="shg">
                <p>
                  <strong>SHG.</strong> Two photons of one beam add to a
                  second-harmonic photon.
                </p>
                <div class="note-eq" {...eqAttrs} set:html={eq(String.raw`\omega+\omega\rightarrow 2\omega,
                  \qquad \lambda_{\mathrm{out}}=\frac{\lambda_1}{2}`)} />
              </div>
              <div data-process="thg" hidden>
                <p>
                  <strong>THG.</strong> The degenerate third-harmonic sum. In a real
                  setup this is usually SHG followed by SFG of the two beams, not a
                  single third-order step.
                </p>
                <div class="note-eq" {...eqAttrs} set:html={eq(String.raw`\omega+\omega+\omega\rightarrow 3\omega,
                  \qquad \lambda_{\mathrm{out}}=\frac{\lambda_1}{3}`)} />
              </div>
              <div data-process="sfg" hidden>
                <p>
                  <strong>SFG.</strong> Two different input photons add; the output is
                  always the shortest wavelength of the three.
                </p>
                <div class="note-eq" {...eqAttrs} set:html={eq(String.raw`\omega_3=\omega_1+\omega_2,
                  \qquad \frac{1}{\lambda_{\mathrm{out}}}=\frac{1}{\lambda_1}+\frac{1}{\lambda_2}`)} />
              </div>
              <div data-process="dfg" hidden>
                <p>
                  <strong>DFG.</strong> The difference frequency. The modulus makes the
                  order of the two inputs irrelevant here.
                </p>
                <div class="note-eq" {...eqAttrs} set:html={eq(String.raw`\omega_3=\left|\omega_1-\omega_2\right|,
                  \qquad \frac{1}{\lambda_{\mathrm{out}}}
                  =\left|\frac{1}{\lambda_1}-\frac{1}{\lambda_2}\right|`)} />
              </div>
              <div data-process="opa" hidden>
                <p>
                  <strong>OPA.</strong> A pump photon splits into signal and idler;
                  <span set:html={im('\\lambda_1')} /> is the pump and
                  <span set:html={im('\\lambda_2')} /> the seeded signal, which must be the
                  longer of the two. The idler is reported as the output.
                </p>
                <div class="note-eq" {...eqAttrs} set:html={eq(String.raw`\omega_{\mathrm{p}}
                  =\omega_{\mathrm{s}}+\omega_{\mathrm{i}},
                  \qquad \frac{1}{\lambda_{\mathrm{p}}}
                  =\frac{1}{\lambda_{\mathrm{s}}}+\frac{1}{\lambda_{\mathrm{i}}}`)} />
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="note-eq" {...eqAttrs} set:html={eq(String.raw`E=\frac{hc}{\lambda},
              \qquad hc=1239.84193\ \mathrm{eV\,nm}`)} />
            <div set:html={defs([
              ['\\lambda_1,\\ \\lambda_2', 'input wavelengths (pump and signal for OPA)'],
              ['\\lambda_{\\mathrm{out}}', 'generated wavelength'],
              ['\\omega', 'angular frequency, 2&pi;c/&lambda;'],
              ['E', 'photon energy of the output'],
            ])} />
            <p class="theory-note"><strong>Assumptions.</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Energy conservation only: phase matching
                (<span set:html={im('\\Delta k=0')} />), the crystal and its transparency
                and damage limits, the conversion efficiency and the finite bandwidth of
                the pulses are all outside this calculator.
              </li>
              <li>
                A wavelength it returns is therefore allowed by energy conservation, not
                necessarily reachable in a given crystal&nbsp;[5].
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============================================================ -->
